{
"dur": 53.519,
"shots": [
{
"i": 1,
"start": 0.0,
"vo_start": 0.6,
"vo_end": 5.96,
"text": "Mayer had five sons, and he did something clever with them. He sent them out across Europe.",
"end": 6.235
},
{
"i": 2,
"start": 6.235,
"vo_start": 6.51,
"vo_end": 16.224,
"text": "Amschel stayed in Frankfurt. Salomon went to Vienna. Nathan to London. Carl to Naples. James to Paris.",
"end": 16.499
},
{
"i": 3,
"start": 16.499,
"vo_start": 16.774,
"vo_end": 23.757,
"text": "Five arrows, tied together. It became the family crest: separate, you can snap one; bundled, they hold.",
"end": 24.032
},
{
"i": 4,
"start": 24.032,
"vo_start": 24.307,
"vo_end": 31.775,
"text": "Brothers in five capitals meant they could move money across borders, by letter and private courier, faster and more safely than almost anyone.",
"end": 32.05
},
{
"i": 5,
"start": 32.05,
"vo_start": 32.325,
"vo_end": 38.165,
"text": "They wrote to each other constantly, sometimes in Yiddish written in Hebrew letters, which few outsiders could read.",
"end": 38.44
},
{
"i": 6,
"start": 38.44,
"vo_start": 38.715,
"vo_end": 45.432,
"text": "If one brother saw trouble coming in his city, the others knew within days. That information was worth a fortune on its own.",
"end": 45.707
},
{
"i": 7,
"start": 45.707,
"vo_start": 45.982,
"vo_end": 52.569,
"text": "Mayer died in 1812. His will told the sons to stay united and keep the business in the family.",
"end": 53.519
}
],
